A||||10||Voytek||Clutch Noise - Need Help||||9||
Z||000000||Voytek||03-13-2001||02:50 PM||||Last year around March my 87 GT developed a nasty noise in the clutch.  When pressing the clutch pedal, the noise would get louder (to a certain point) and disappear after engaging the clutch.  The noise was like a loud ticking/banging varying with the speed of engine RPM and accompanied by some clutch vibration (could be felt in the pedal when engaging the clutch).  Even though the car ran fine, I decided it's time for a new clutch.  After spending $1300 CDN (about $1.00 USD) and acquiring a new clutch/pressure plate/release bearing/fork/bushings/retainer/master cylinder) the noise disappeared and the car was fairly smooth.  Well, not even 15000 kms later (about 9,000 miles) the EXACT same problem is back!  All mechanics I talked to (then and now) are baffled and don't have a clue.  The shop is telling me there is no warranty on a FIERO clutch.  Has anyone ever heard of such problem?  Any ideas?  Please help!||||reg||9
Z||000001||skitime||03-14-2001||11:37 AM||||Okay, you made me feel guilty since it was my girlfriend that got all the posts. I will give you probable causes directly from the factory manual.<P>Rattling - Transmission Click<P>A. Release fork loose - Install properly<BR>B. Release bearing binding - Clean, relubricate, check for burrs, nicks, ect.<BR>C. Driven plate damper spring failure - Replace driven disc.<BR>D. Low engine idle speed - Adjust idle speed<P>Noisy<P>A. Worn release bearing - replace bearing<BR>B. Fork shaft improperly installed - Install properly and lubricate fork fingers at bearing<P>I only have automatics (3) but start looking at these possible problems.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FST2M6||03-14-2001||11:43 AM||||ok.. the input shaft bearings on our trannys wasn't really a good idead.. it's light duty ball unit that needs to be replaced with a needle bearin/race/housing. my 3.65 had a problem similar.. but my clutch just broke when the shaft would shift. I was lucky and never split the case. While i was in ther ei just got a hold of Mr. Fiero and ordered a 4:10 tranny from him a rebuilt it.. the rebuild was about $200USD and i installed it my self. another thing.. the bronze bushings that the clutch fork sits on wear down and can/will cause noise vibration and clutch failure and managament difficulty (sticks in or out) they become elongated and change what used to be a good leverage point into a bad one... those bushing are available at NAPA for about $10USD. Looks like your gunna have to dive back into the bell housing...  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||stimpy||03-14-2001||12:13 PM||||No warranty on a Fiero clutch?! WTF is that? Did this shop make it crystal clear that they would not honor their warranty BEFORE they took your money?  If not, I think courts would find that clear breach of contract. Fight the power!<P>BTW, Nice tits, Voytek!<P>------------------<BR>Loud Pipes Save Lives!      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/stimpy_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Alex4mula||03-14-2001||12:15 PM||||Looks like something is wearing your release bearing very fast. Check your driving habits. If you run the clutch it will wear a lot faster. 1FST post is very accurate. I had many problems in mine until I fixed those bushings and shaft.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Gold-86SE||03-14-2001||12:42 PM||||<BR>I tend to think it is something external that is probably the cause.  Not so much your driving habits, unless you frequently dump the clutch for hard launches.<P>I tend to think you need to replace all of your tranny mounts, and engine mounts.  If the tranny and engine do not sit in proper specs the clutch is skewed and operates with rubbing angles instead of a smooth straight movement on the clutch.<P>I just replaced my clutch and let me tell you my tranny mounts and motor mounts were shot, kaput, dead, and useless.  Had I not replaced them in the process, my clutch work would have been for nothing.<P>------------------<BR>84 SE 4.10 4 Speed (Down and Out for the Count)<BR>85 GT Automatic (Restoration Project)<BR>86 SE 4 Speed (Fair Weather Daily Driver)<BR>87 SC 5 Speed (Not Running, V8 Conversion?)<BR>Please visit my <A HREF="http://www.trifocus.net/~fws/index.html" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Work Shop</A>||||reg||1
Z||000006||DJRice||03-14-2001||11:04 PM||||Stimpy, LOL!<P>You probably should go through the clutch hydraulics just to be on the safe side, but I would bet your problem lies in the fork assembly or throwout bearing.  Its definitely greasy time...<P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6||||reg||1
Z||000007||Voytek||03-15-2001||12:23 PM||||Thanks for all the help. A lot of valuable info.  I DON'T ride the clutch, but that's besides the point now.  It actually turned out to be a bearing on the crank.  The transmission shop didn't know sh*t.  An engine shop determined that w/o taking the cradle out.  Now it's finally time for a 3.1.  The tranny shop DID NOT tell me there is no warranty on the Fiero clutch before commencing the work.  They told me when I was picking up the car.  The clutch used is a LUK clutch.  I e-mailed the manufacturer and was told that a std. warranty on ALL their clutches is 1 yr, 12,000 miles EXCEPT the Fiero (??!!!!).  Anyways, ask your shop before fixing your clutch and make sure they don't use a LUK.<BR>Thanks again.||||reg||1
Z||000008||1FST2M6||03-15-2001||01:33 PM||||LUK, Sachs, borg & beck beck arnely all the stock clutch makers don't warranty the Fiero Clutch. Centerforce does though if you can prove its their fault!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Ram doens't warranty thier units either.. but they'll take it back and refund your $.. ??WTF?? i was really happy with my ACT though. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Chance||03-15-2001||11:35 PM||||DON'T TELL MY MECHANIC THEY DON'T WARRENTY FIERO CLUTCHS, because before I got it done, he said "12 months/12,000 miles" and told me again when I picked it up!!! (his place is an NAPA service center) but I just got my 86gt 5sp back after him having it a month, and the clutch not working right, or at all for a year!||||reg||4
Z||000010||lowCG||03-16-2001||12:40 AM||||There was a factory bulletin that went out to GM dealer's service departments stating that the fiero clutch cannot be truly guaranteed unless everything is replaced from the pedal bushings,all hydraulics,release fork/bushings...<BR>Manufacturers followed apparently.<BR>Your problem sounds like the bearing/fork.<BR>||||reg||1
